This uses a breadth-first walk to save every node's parent. After that, the depths of `p` and `q` are found. The deeper node is moved upward until both nodes are at the same level, then both move upward together until they meet.

**Dry run:** if `p` is two levels below `q`, `p` moves up twice first. From there, equal upward steps keep the nodes together by level, and their first meeting point is the answer.

The root is stored with a `nil` parent, so a target that is the root is handled without a special tree structure.

```go
/*
// TreeNode definition for the binary tree
type TreeNode struct {
	Data  int
	Left  *TreeNode
	Right *TreeNode
}
*/

func lowestCommonAncestor(root, p, q *TreeNode) *TreeNode {
	if root == nil || p == nil || q == nil {
		return nil
	}

	parent := map[*TreeNode]*TreeNode{root: nil}
	queue := []*TreeNode{root}

	for len(queue) > 0 && (parent[p] == nil && p != root || parent[q] == nil && q != root) {
		node := queue[0]
		queue = queue[1:]

		if node.Left != nil {
			parent[node.Left] = node
			queue = append(queue, node.Left)
		}
		if node.Right != nil {
			parent[node.Right] = node
			queue = append(queue, node.Right)
		}
	}

	if _, ok := parent[p]; !ok {
		return nil
	}
	if _, ok := parent[q]; !ok {
		return nil
	}

	depth := func(node *TreeNode) int {
		levels := 0
		for node != root {
			node = parent[node]
			levels++
		}
		return levels
	}

	depthP, depthQ := depth(p), depth(q)
	for depthP > depthQ {
		p = parent[p]
		depthP--
	}
	for depthQ > depthP {
		q = parent[q]
		depthQ--
	}

	for p != q {
		p = parent[p]
		q = parent[q]
	}
	return p
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` for the walk and upward moves.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the parent map and queue.

This version first stores the path from the root to each target. The last node that appears in both paths is the lowest common ancestor. Comparing node references is important here, since two different nodes can have the same value.

**Dry run:** for paths `[root, left, p]` and `[root, right, q]`, only `root` matches before the paths split, so `root` is returned.

The empty-tree case returns `null`, and when `p` and `q` are the same object, the common path ends at that node.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        List<TreeNode> pathToP = new ArrayList<>();
        List<TreeNode> pathToQ = new ArrayList<>();

        if (!findPath(root, p, pathToP) || !findPath(root, q, pathToQ)) {
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode answer = null;
        int limit = Math.min(pathToP.size(), pathToQ.size());

        for (int i = 0; i < limit; i++) {
            if (pathToP.get(i) != pathToQ.get(i)) {
                break;
            }
            answer = pathToP.get(i);
        }

        return answer;
    }

    private boolean findPath(TreeNode root, TreeNode target, List<TreeNode> path) {
        if (root == null) {
            return false;
        }

        path.add(root);
        if (root == target) {
            return true;
        }

        if (findPath(root.left, target, path) || findPath(root.right, target, path)) {
            return true;
        }

        path.remove(path.size() - 1);
        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` for the two searches and path comparison.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the stored paths and recursion stack.

Instead of saving paths, this version visits children before their parent with an explicit stack. Each node gets a count showing how many target nodes are inside its subtree. When the count reaches two, that node is the answer.

This avoids recursion depth problems on a very tall tree. The same-node case is checked first, because one target should count as both targets there.

```csharp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     public int val;
 *     public TreeNode left;
 *     public TreeNode right;
 *     public TreeNode(int val=0, TreeNode left=null, TreeNode right=null) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */

using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public TreeNode LowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        if (root == null || p == null || q == null) {
            return null;
        }
        if (object.ReferenceEquals(p, q)) {
            return p;
        }

        var counts = new Dictionary<TreeNode, int>();
        var stack = new Stack<(TreeNode node, bool expanded)>();
        stack.Push((root, false));

        while (stack.Count > 0) {
            var item = stack.Pop();
            TreeNode node = item.node;

            if (!item.expanded) {
                stack.Push((node, true));
                if (node.right != null) {
                    stack.Push((node.right, false));
                }
                if (node.left != null) {
                    stack.Push((node.left, false));
                }
                continue;
            }

            int count = object.ReferenceEquals(node, p) || object.ReferenceEquals(node, q) ? 1 : 0;
            if (node.left != null) {
                count += counts[node.left];
            }
            if (node.right != null) {
                count += counts[node.right];
            }

            counts[node] = count;
            if (count == 2) {
                return node;
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with one processing step per node.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and subtree counts. The input tree is not changed.

A short recursive version works well here. If the current node is either target, it is returned. Then the left and right results tell whether the two targets were found in different branches. When both sides return a node, the current node is the answer.

**Dry run:** if `p` is found in the left subtree and `q` in the right subtree, the current root receives two non-null results and is returned as their first shared ancestor. If both are in the same side, that side's result moves upward.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
public:
    TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {
        if (root == nullptr || root == p || root == q) {
            return root;
        }

        TreeNode* leftResult = lowestCommonAncestor(root->left, p, q);
        TreeNode* rightResult = lowestCommonAncestor(root->right, p, q);

        if (leftResult != nullptr && rightResult != nullptr) {
            return root;
        }

        return leftResult != nullptr ? leftResult : rightResult;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each node is checked once.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursion stack, where `h` is the tree height.

